//? Title: MOEA/D decomposition
//? Subtitle: Actual neighborhoods, scalar replacements and the original ZDT1 front
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en-GB

\abstract(
Minimizing several conflicting objectives produces a set of tradeoffs rather
than a single best answer. MOEA/D approximates that set through neighboring
scalar subproblems. We derive its scalarizations, inspect actual child
replacements, retain the original discrete weight illustration and
thirty-variable ZDT1 experiment, and qualify front-gap and computational-cost
claims using the implementation’s real setup and output filtering.
)

\toc

> Decompose a vector objective

A feasible point dominates another when every minimized objective is no larger
and at least one is strictly smaller. MOEA/D assigns each population slot a
simplex weight, then optimizes nearby weights together. The native Tchebycheff
score anchors to the current ideal point z, the componentwise minimum seen after
initialization. It uses a positive floor on zero weights.

$$
g_{te}(f\mid w,z)=\max_k\{\max(w_k,10^{-6})|f_k-z_k|\},\qquad g_{ws}(f\mid w,z)=\sum_k w_k(f_k-z_k).
$$
\alttext(Tchebycheff uses the largest weighted deviation from the ideal; weighted sum adds weighted deviations.)

Weights come from the Das-Dennis simplex lattice, with H divisions and
binomial(H+m−1,m−1) rows. The code chooses the largest lattice fitting
max(requested population,m), exactly the requested count for two objectives.
Each slot's neighborhood consists of its closest weights in Euclidean distance,
including itself. Equal-distance ordering follows the native standard sort
rather than a promised portable tie rule.

At each sequential subproblem visit, two parents are sampled with replacement
from its neighborhood and can coincide. Shared SBX crossover produces two
children; only the first is mutated and evaluated. The ideal is updated before
scalar comparisons. The child replaces every visited neighbor whose score it
ties or improves, using that neighbor's weight. Updates take effect immediately,
so later visits can use already replaced parents. There is no external archive.

> Read an actual child and its neighbor decisions

A five-slot, two-variable ZDT1 run with two generations is small enough to
inspect. Its first child and three neighbor comparisons come directly from the
producer. Both candidate and incumbent scores use the same already updated
ideal. The diagram labels each actual replacement decision, avoiding a
fabricated optimization history.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/moead_tiny.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
#include <datamunge/optim/moead.hpp>
#include <datamunge/plot/diagram.hpp>
#include <filesystem>
#include <print>
int main() {
    std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
    dm::optim::MOEADOptions o;
    o.population_size = 5;
    o.neighborhood_size = 3;
    o.max_generations = 2;
    o.seed = 1;
    dm::optim::ZDT1Objective f(2);
    auto t = dm::optim::MOEAD(o).trace(f, {0, 0}, {1, 1});
    auto &s = t.steps.front();
    dm::graphics::Diagram d;
    d.node("child", {0, 3},
           std::format("Actual first child\nf=({:.4f}, {:.4f})",
                       s.child.objectives[0], s.child.objectives[1]));
    std::print("Actual parents: {}, {}. Actual callback count: {}.\n\n| neighbor slot | incumbent score | " "child score | replaced |\n| ---: | ---: | ---: | --- |\n",
               s.parent_a, s.parent_b, t.evaluations);
    for (std::size_t i = 0; i < s.replacements.size(); ++i) {
        auto &r = s.replacements[i];
        std::print("| {} | {} | {} | {} |\n", r.neighbor, r.incumbent_score,
                   r.child_score, r.replaced);
        auto id = std::to_string(i);
        d.node(id, {8, double(i) * 3},
               std::format("Actual neighbor {}\nreplacement {}", r.neighbor,
                           r.replaced)).edge("child", id);
    }
    d.save_svg("build/moead_tiny.svg");
}
```
