> Retain the original discrete weight illustration

The original five candidate objective pairs
are(0,1),(0.25,0.5),(0.5,0.293),(0.75,0.134),(1,0). Two values are rounded
approximations to the ZDT1 curve. The five original weights and the fixed
ideal(0,0) are retained in the native demonstration helper. It selects the first
strict minimum over this discrete list. These choices illustrate scalar
preference and are neither continuous global minimizers nor a solver population.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/moead_weights.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
#include <datamunge/optim/moead.hpp>
#include <datamunge/plot/plot.hpp>
#include <filesystem>
#include <print>
int main() {
    std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
    auto rows = dm::optim::moead_original_choices();
    std::vector<double> x, y;
    std::print("| weight1 | weight2 | chosen f1 | chosen rounded f2 | actual score |\n| ---: | ---: | ---: " "| ---: | ---: |\n");
    for (auto &r : rows) {
        x.push_back(r.objectives[0]);
        y.push_back(r.objectives[1]);
        std::print("| {} | {} | {} | {} | {} |\n", r.weight[0], r.weight[1],
                   r.objectives[0], r.objectives[1], r.score);
    }
    auto p = dm::graphics::RPlot::create();
    p.points(x, y, "discrete choices", {37, 99, 235}, 7).title(
        "Original weights: discrete candidate choices").x_label(
        "chosen objective1").y_label("chosen objective2").save_svg(
        "build/moead_weights.svg");
}
```
