> Contracts, conclusion and references

Bounds must be nonempty, finite, matching and strictly ordered with
representable widths. Probabilities, distribution indices and decomposition
names are validated before callbacks. Requested population is2..1000000;
objective count must be fixed, finite and at least two, checked after its actual
evaluation. Invalid shapes, nonfinite values, unrepresentable scalar arithmetic
and count geometry throw; earlier callback effects remain. Parent sampling with
replacement and the discarded probe preserve valid legacy scheduling.

The optional trace owns options, bounds, probe, weights, neighborhoods,
initial/final populations, ideal points and all actual offspring/replacement
records. Its quota counts stored vector rows and elements, point records and
coordinates/objectives, ideal scalars and decision records; default1000000, zero
disables. It is an online storage limit, not a transactional callback budget:
earlier evaluations may have happened when it fails. No partial trace is
returned.

The actual scalar decisions explain the mechanism, while the original experiment
measures finite coverage and error. Neither a small mean gap nor a decomposition
argument establishes a global Pareto-set guarantee or a universal advantage over
other evolutionary methods.

[Zhang and Li, MOEA/D: A multiobjective evolutionary algorithm based on decomposition (2007)|https://doi.org/10.1109/TEVC.2007.892759]
introduces the framework.
[Das and Dennis, Normal-boundary intersection (1998)|https://doi.org/10.1137/S1052623496307510]
describes the simplex-lattice context.
[Zitzler, Deb and Thiele, Comparison of multiobjective evolutionary algorithms: Empirical results (2000)|https://doi.org/10.1162/106365600568202]
defines the benchmark family.

> Verification

The ten-report batch passes95 CTest cases,31 directly instrumented ASan/UBSan
cases,83 compiled and executed public examples,363/363 scoped documentation
definitions and ten representative hovers. Independent Python integer
arithmetic, MT19937-64/variation replay, graph sets, analytic interpolation,
circle boundary enumeration and actual-sample integration reconstruction agree
on915459 captured records. All six original RMS summaries are separately checked
using96 native MISER estimates and1600000 independently reproduced
plain-comparator points. Frozen valid-input comparisons include the original
seeded solver paths and full MOEA/D fronts. Ten checkout headers and ten
identically installed development headers compile independently, and an
installed-header native consumer links and runs. Each report has four executed
complete programs, four reviewed native figures and both HTML/Markdown exports
with computed-result and image-byte checks. This is a scoped native API audit,
not an installation claim for every language binding. Receipts:
`build/mepml-conversion/batch-234-243/`.
